### name: norm.size.result-past-the-budget-is-refused-where-the-walk-stops
--- note
The result expression is charged as the translator walks it (§7.4) and refused once the count
passes the budget (E_SQL_SIZE). Code only: sql/errors.md gives the code no position.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"XN": {"kind": "column", "column": "xn", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
X0 = XN; X1 = X0 + X0; X2 = X1 + X1; X3 = X2 + X2; X4 = X3 + X3; X5 = X4 + X4; X6 = X5 + X5; X7 = X6 + X6; X8 = X7 + X7; X9 = X8 + X8; X10 = X9 + X9; X11 = X10 + X10; X12 = X11 + X11; X13 = X12 + X12; X14 = X13 + X13; X15 = X14 + X14; X16 = X15 + X15; X16 + X16 > 0
--- error
E_SQL_SIZE
===

### name: refuse.in-relation.boolean-needle-at-the-needle
--- note
IN over a relation compares as `==` does: a BOOL needle is refused at the needle, not at the
operator.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"XB": {"kind": "column", "column": "xb", "type": "BOOL"}, "TS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "ts", "alias": "ts", "fields": {"V": {"table": "ts", "column": "v", "type": "TEXT"}}, "scalar": "V"}}
--- source
XB IN TS
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===

### name: refuse.binder.checked-at-the-call-not-in-stage-1
--- note
GO-C2: a binder position that is not a bare name is refused when the call is translated, so
a refusal the call meets first (MAP as JOIN's source) wins. Refusing the binder earlier, in stage 1, would hide it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"XB": {"kind": "column", "column": "xb", "type": "BOOL"}}
--- source
JOIN(MAP((1, 2), 1 + 1, "a"), ",") == "a" AND XB
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:6
===

### name: plan.pure-memory.key-read-after-a-filter-is-not-split
--- note
keysObservable: a split directly after a FILTER hands the continuation renumbered keys, so a
continuation step that mentions `_K` anywhere keeps the plan in memory (argsReadKey is a raw
scan); a scope-aware test would split here.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "t", "fields": {"N": {"table": "t", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}, "S": {"table": "t", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
T .> FILTER(_["n"] > 1) .> MAP(RECORD("x", SPLIT(_["s"], ","), "y", ANY((1, 2), _K == "1")))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
t
===

### name: bind.prefilter.unknown-spelling-is-refused
--- note
GO-C19: an unknown prefilter spelling is refused when the binding is made. Storing it would defer the error to a later use.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"XC": {"kind": "column", "column": "xc", "type": "TEXT", "prefilter": "bogus"}}
--- source
XC == "a"
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

### name: order.filter-after-sort-keeps-the-order
--- note
Found by an audit: `ORDERS .> SORT_BY(amount) .> FILTER(amount > 1)` used to emit
`SELECT _sub1.* FROM (SELECT o.* ... ORDER BY amount) _sub1 WHERE ...` in every host: a
derived table with no LIMIT beside its ORDER BY does not keep the order, and nothing
outside it sorts, so the rows came back in no order. The WHERE goes beside the ORDER BY in
the same select instead (a filter commutes with a stable sort).
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["amount"]) .> FILTER(_["amount"] > 1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`amount` > 1) ORDER BY `o`.`amount` ASC
===

### name: order.projection-then-filter-of-sorted-rows-is-refused
--- note
The wrap cannot be avoided when a projection sits between (the filter reads the projected
columns), and the derived table then loses the sort: the statement is refused at the last
step, and the hybrid planner keeps the sorted rows in SQL and filters in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["amount"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"])) .> FILTER(_["a"] > 1)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:68
===

### name: bind.alias.case-colliding-relation-aliases-are-refused
--- note
SQLite reads the aliases `o` and `O` as one alias (`ambiguous column name: o.x`), and the MySQL family
does on some platforms, so two relations under aliases that differ only by ASCII case collide on the
server exactly as two under the same alias do (PHP-C57). Refused with the same E_SQL_BINDING.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "relation", "from": "a", "alias": "o", "fields": {"X": {"table": "o", "column": "x", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "B": {"kind": "relation", "from": "b", "alias": "O", "fields": {"X": {"table": "O", "column": "x", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
COUNT(A) > 0
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===

### name: assign.empty-text-key-is-a-constant-index
--- note
GO-C40: `""` is a text literal like any other, so an index written `R[""]` is a constant key; Go
treated it as "not constant" and refused the program at the assignment target with E_SQL_ASSIGN,
where JS refuses later with E_SQL_SHAPE. Both refuse, but the code is a contract: the target is a
legal constant index, and the refusal that follows is the one for reading a key of a value that has
no SQL form (a record built by assignment).
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"XN": {"kind": "column", "column": "xn", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
R[""] = 1; R[""] > XN
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===

### name: plan.order.sort-over-a-projection-of-sorted-rows-stays-in-memory
--- note
Gate fuzz (seed 4243, program 414): the statement is refused for the sort over the projection of sorted rows (docs/internals/sql-translation.md 12.1: "a sort over a projection of sorted rows" loses the earlier sort's order as tie-break), and the contract says the planner "keeps the sorted rows in SQL and runs the step in memory". So the prefix ends at the projection: JS, C++ and Go pushed the refused sort (`ORDER BY amount DESC` in an outer select) into the prefix, PHP, Python and Lisp did not. The LIMIT beside the inner ORDER BY keeps that order, so the derived table is allowed.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TOP_BY(_["id"], 2) .> SELECT_COLS("id", "amount") .> SORT_BY(_["amount"], "DESC") .> TOP_BY(_K, 2)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.`id`, `_sub1`.`amount` FROM (SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` ASC LIMIT 2) `_sub1`
===

### name: plan.order.limit-then-link-stays-in-memory
--- note
A LINK after a TOP_BY: the LIMIT beside the ORDER BY decides which rows survive, not the order a join returns them in (docs/internals/sql-translation.md 12.1, "Order": a join over a derived table loses the order). The prefix ends before the LINK; the LINK runs in memory over the limited, sorted rows.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CNAME": {"table": "c", "column": "cname", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TOP_BY(_["id"], 3) .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"]))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` ASC LIMIT 3
===

### name: plan.order.link-left-over-sorted-rows-stays-in-memory
--- note
LINK_LEFT is the same step as LINK for this rule: sorted rows come from SQL, the join runs in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CNAME": {"table": "c", "column": "cname", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["amount"]) .> LINK_LEFT(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"]))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`amount` ASC
===

### name: plan.order.projection-then-link-over-sorted-rows-stays-in-memory
--- note
A projection between the sort and the LINK does not bring the order back: the LINK is still over sorted rows. The sorted, projected rows come from SQL; the LINK runs in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CNAME": {"table": "c", "column": "cname", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["amount"]) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _["customer_id"], "a", _["amount"])) .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["a"]))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id` AS `cid`, `o`.`amount` AS `a` FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`amount` ASC
===

### name: plan.order.sort-filter-then-link-stays-in-memory
--- note
A FILTER after the sort keeps the ORDER BY in the same select (12.1); a LINK after it is over sorted rows, so it runs in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CNAME": {"table": "c", "column": "cname", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["amount"]) .> FILTER(_["amount"] > 1) .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"]))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`amount` > 1) ORDER BY `o`.`amount` ASC
===


### name: plan.keys.filter-filter-then-a-renumbering-step-splits-after-the-first
--- note
Gate fuzz (seed 4243, program 338). The prefix may end at a FILTER when the continuation cannot tell the database renumbered the rows (docs/internals/sql-translation.md 12.1, "Row keys"): the continuation's first step here is a FILTER, which keeps the keys it is handed, but the SORT after it renumbers and nothing reads `_K` before it, so the retained keys never reach the answer. JS, PHP, Python, Lisp and Go split after the first FILTER; C++ stopped at any FILTER that started the continuation and planned pure_memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["amount"] == 2) .> FILTER(_["nope"]["x"] $== "b") .> SORT()
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`amount` = 2)
===

### name: plan.keys.filter-filter-then-a-key-read-stays-in-memory
--- note
The control: the step after the continuation's FILTER renumbers, but it reads `_K` first -- the keys the FILTER kept, which the database's rowset does not have -- so there is no split point and nothing pushes down.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["amount"] == 2) .> FILTER(_["nope"]["x"] $== "b") .> SORT_BY(_K)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===
